\section{Introduction}

The rapid proliferation of Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT)
systems has enabled continuous monitoring of smart
manufacturing, industrial automation, and critical
infrastructure, generating massive volumes of numerical sensor
data~\cite{ref1,ref2}. To reduce storage and management costs,
these data are commonly outsourced to cloud platforms.
However, because cloud servers are generally considered
semi-honest, sensitive operational information must be
protected before outsourcing~\cite{ref3,ref4}. Although
encryption preserves confidentiality, it also makes efficient
query processing and analytics considerably more challenging.

Searchable encryption (SE) enables secure retrieval over
encrypted data without exposing plaintext to the cloud
server~\cite{ref3,ref4}. Existing SE and attribute-based
searchable encryption (ABSE)
schemes~\cite{ref5,ref6,ref7,ref8,ref9,ref10,ref11}
primarily support keyword-based retrieval. Recent advances
have extended these schemes with dynamic searchable
encryption, multi-keyword search, fine-grained access control,
and forward/backward privacy~\cite{ref12}, while further
improving verifiable retrieval~\cite{ref13,ref14,ref15,ref16,ref17},
search-pattern privacy~\cite{ref18}, update
privacy~\cite{ref19,ref20}, and privacy-preserving trapdoor
generation~\cite{ref19}. Nevertheless, practical IIoT
applications require efficient support for
\emph{multi-dimensional numerical range queries} together with
real-time statistical analytics over encrypted sensor data,
capabilities that remain largely unsupported by existing
schemes.

For example, a factory operator may retrieve all sensor
records satisfying

\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{Temperature}&\in[60^\circ\mathrm{C},80^\circ\mathrm{C}]
\\
\land\;
\mathrm{Vibration}&\in[10,20]
\\
\land\;
\mathrm{Pressure}&\in[40,55],
\end{aligned}
\]

while simultaneously computing aggregate statistics such as
the sum, count, or average of the matched records without
revealing either the query or the underlying sensor values.

Supporting such secure IIoT analytics remains challenging for
three reasons. First, existing range-query
schemes~\cite{ref20,ref21,ref22,ref23,ref24,ref25,ref26,ref27,ref28,ref29,ref30,ref31,ref32}
primarily optimize encrypted retrieval encrypted retrieval and generally do not
integrate encrypted aggregation. Second, searchable encryption and privacy-preserving
aggregation are typically designed as independent components,
requiring additional communication and computation for
post-query aggregation. Meanwhile, blockchain-assisted secure
outsourcing and encrypted computation have also been
investigated for cloud and edge environments~\cite{ref13,ref17,ref33}.
However, these approaches do not tightly integrate searchable
retrieval with privacy-preserving aggregation within a unified
encrypted analytics framework~\cite{ref13,ref15,ref16,ref17,ref18,ref19,ref20,ref34}.
Third, existing verifiable searchable encryption mainly
authenticates retrieval correctness but does not jointly
guarantee authenticated query-state integrity, completeness,
freshness, and cryptographic binding between aggregation
results and the verified matched record set. Consequently, despite substantial progress in searchable
encryption, dynamic searchable encryption,
blockchain-assisted verification, and encrypted range-query
processing~\cite{ref13,ref9,ref12,ref33,ref29,ref30,ref31,ref32,ref35},
providing efficient, authenticated, and privacy-preserving
encrypted analytics for outsourced IIoT data remains an open
problem.

To address these challenges, we propose
\emph{Blockchain-based Verifiable Conjunctive Range Search
and Aggregation (BVCRSA)}, a unified framework for secure and
verifiable encrypted IIoT analytics. BVCRSA combines an
authenticated hierarchical range-cover index, protected bitmap
filtering, and cross-dimensional bitmap intersection to
support efficient conjunctive range search. It further
integrates attribute-based searchable encryption with
Threshold EC-ElGamal homomorphic encryption to enable
fine-grained authorized aggregation directly over encrypted
records. Finally, a blockchain-assisted Verifiable
Query-State Anchoring (VQSA) mechanism authenticates
searchable-index states, bitmap commitments, and aggregation
metadata through Merkle-based verification, providing
authenticated integrity, completeness, freshness, rollback
resistance, and cryptographic binding between aggregation
ciphertexts and the verified matched record set without
requiring zero-knowledge proofs or trusted hardware.

The main contributions of this paper are summarized as
follows:

\begin{itemize}

\item We propose \textbf{BVCRSA}, a unified framework that
jointly supports verifiable conjunctive range search,
fine-grained access control, encrypted aggregation, and
blockchain-assisted query-state verification over outsourced
IIoT data.

\item We design an \textbf{authenticated hierarchical
range-cover indexing mechanism} that combines canonical
interval decomposition with protected bitmap filtering and
cross-dimensional bitmap intersection to enable efficient
multi-dimensional range-query processing.

\item We develop a \textbf{bitmap-aware encrypted aggregation
mechanism} that tightly couples searchable indexing with
Threshold EC-ElGamal homomorphic encryption, enabling direct
computation of sum, count, and average statistics over
authorized query results without exposing aggregation keys.

\item We introduce a \textbf{Verifiable Query-State Anchoring
(VQSA)} mechanism that authenticates searchable-index states,
bitmap commitments, and aggregation metadata using compact
blockchain-anchored Merkle commitments, providing query-state
integrity, authenticated completeness, freshness, rollback
resistance, and cryptographic binding between aggregation
ciphertexts and the verified matched record set.

\end{itemize}


