\subsection{Verifiable Searchable Encryption and Attribute-Based Search}

Verifiable searchable encryption (VSE) enables users to
verify the correctness and integrity of outsourced search
results, while attribute-based searchable encryption (ABSE)
provides fine-grained access control. Early
ABSE schemes~\cite{ref6} introduced attribute-constrained
search, and subsequent studies extended them with
hierarchical search~\cite{ref36}, hidden access
policies~\cite{ref37}, multi-keyword
search~\cite{ref5,ref38,ref7},
blockchain-assisted auditability~\cite{ref39,ref40},
revocation~\cite{ref41,ref42}, and resistance to
keyword-guessing and leakage
attacks~\cite{ref43,ref44,ref45}. Nevertheless, these
schemes remain primarily keyword-oriented.

Recent VSE
schemes~\cite{ref13,ref14,ref15,ref16,ref17,ref35}
have strengthened authenticated query processing through
query-result verification, secure delegation, provenance
validation, and blockchain-assisted integrity assurance.
Dynamic searchable encryption further improves
multi-query search and
revocation~\cite{ref8}, forward/backward
privacy~\cite{ref46,ref47}, update
privacy~\cite{ref19,ref20}, and
privacy-preserving trapdoor
generation~\cite{ref18,ref19}. Despite these advances,
existing schemes remain predominantly keyword-oriented and
do not jointly support authenticated conjunctive numerical
range search and privacy-preserving encrypted aggregation,
leaving efficient verifiable numerical analytics largely
unexplored.

\subsection{Privacy-Preserving Range Query}

Privacy-preserving range-query processing has been widely
studied for encrypted databases and IoT systems.
Order-preserving and order-revealing
encryption~\cite{ref48} support efficient ciphertext
comparison but inherently leak ordering information.
Existing encrypted range-query
schemes~\cite{ref21,ref20,ref22} improve secure retrieval
for numerical and spatial data but provide only limited
support for conjunctive multi-dimensional queries and do
not incorporate encrypted analytics. Learned-index
approaches~\cite{ref15} further improve retrieval
efficiency but primarily optimize indexing performance
rather than integrating access control, aggregation, or
authenticated verification.

Several studies focus on improving the scalability of
encrypted range search. Volume-Hiding
SSE~\cite{ref23} reduces information leakage during
retrieval, Revocable DSSE~\cite{ref24} supports dynamic
healthcare-oriented search, Scheme~\cite{ref25} enables
efficient multidimensional range queries, and
Trinity~\cite{ref26} introduces forward-secure
spatio-temporal search using SHVE and quotient filters.
Although these schemes improve search efficiency and query
expressiveness, they generally treat retrieval as an
independent task and do not provide integrated encrypted
aggregation or authenticated result verification.

Recent efforts have combined range queries with
attribute-based and post-quantum searchable encryption.
ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27} supports numerical range queries
under attribute-based authorization but incurs increasing
trapdoor complexity as the query range expands.
Lattice-based searchable
encryption~\cite{ref28} provides post-quantum security
under LWE assumptions but introduces substantially higher
computational overhead due to expensive lattice
operations, limiting practicality for resource-constrained
IIoT deployments.

\subsection{Searchable Encryption with Privacy-Preserving Aggregation}

Homomorphic encryption enables computations to be performed
directly over encrypted data and has recently been combined
with searchable encryption to support secure cloud
analytics. The verifiable searchable encryption
framework~\cite{ref13} integrates partial homomorphic
encryption, B$+$-Tree indexing, bitmap filtering, and
blockchain-assisted verification for Boolean search.
SEaaS~\cite{ref34} combines searchable encryption with
homomorphic computation for encrypted analytics, while
other studies~\cite{ref18,ref19,ref20} improve query
privacy, update privacy, and multi-user searchable
encryption.

Despite these advances, existing schemes typically optimize
either search efficiency, encrypted aggregation, or
verifiable query processing, rather than integrating all
three within a unified framework. Encrypted aggregation is
commonly performed as a separate post-processing step,
incurring additional computation and communication
overhead. Moreover, most verification mechanisms focus on
retrieval correctness without simultaneously ensuring
aggregation integrity, authenticated completeness,
freshness, and rollback resistance.

Table~\ref{tab:feature_comparison} summarizes the
functional capabilities of representative schemes.
Verifiable searchable encryption
schemes~\cite{ref13,ref16,ref17} provide authenticated
search, but Refs.~\cite{ref13,ref16} do not support
numerical range queries, while Ref.~\cite{ref17} lacks
encrypted aggregation. Range-query
schemes~\cite{ref23,ref24,ref25,ref26,ref27,ref28}
improve retrieval efficiency but generally treat search,
aggregation, and verification independently. Conversely,
SEaaS~\cite{ref34} supports encrypted aggregation but
lacks conjunctive range search, fine-grained access
control, and verifiable query processing.

In contrast, BVCRSA introduces a query-centric
architecture that cryptographically binds authenticated
hierarchical range-cover indexing, protected bitmap
filtering, bitmap-aware encrypted aggregation, and
blockchain-assisted query-state verification within a
single authenticated query workflow. Consequently, it
enables efficient conjunctive range search, fine-grained
access control, privacy-preserving aggregation, and
authenticated query-state integrity, canonical-node
completeness, freshness, and rollback resistance for
outsourced IIoT analytics.


\section{Our Proposed Scheme}
\subsection{System Model}
\label{subsec:system_model}

The proposed BVCRSA architecture comprises seven entities,
as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:system_model}. It enables
privacy-preserving conjunctive range search, encrypted
aggregation, threshold-secure aggregate decryption, and
blockchain-anchored result verification over outsourced
IIoT data. Unlike conventional approaches that decrypt data
at the edge, BVCRSA performs encryption directly at the
sensor. Each sensor derives authenticated canonical-node
metadata for indexing, while the Edge Gateway constructs
the authenticated searchable index without accessing
plaintext sensor records or aggregation ciphertexts.

\begin{enumerate}

\item \textbf{IIoT Sensor Nodes.}
Resource-constrained sensors collect industrial
measurements, derive authenticated indexing metadata,
encrypt sensor records using AES-GCM, encrypt numerical
values using Threshold EC-ElGamal, and generate
authentication tags before outsourcing.

\item \textbf{Edge Gateway.}
The Edge Gateway is an honest-but-curious, non-colluding
entity that authenticates sensor data, constructs the
authenticated range-cover index, generates protected
bitmaps and aggregation metadata, and produces
blockchain-bound authenticated commitments. It does not
participate in decryption.

\item \textbf{Cloud Server.}
The Cloud Server stores encrypted data and executes
search, bitmap filtering, and homomorphic aggregation. It
may attempt to infer information from outsourced data or
deviate from the protocol by omitting, modifying, replaying,
or fabricating query results, which are detected through
authenticated batch commitments, Merkle proofs, and
blockchain verification.

\item \textbf{Blockchain Network.}
The blockchain serves as an immutable commitment layer for
authenticated query states, providing integrity,
freshness, and rollback resistance without storing sensor
data or searchable indexes on-chain.

\item \textbf{Data User.}
Authorized users generate attribute-based trapdoors,
submit encrypted conjunctive range queries, verify
authenticated query responses, and request threshold
decryption of aggregate ciphertexts.

\item \textbf{Threshold Decryption Authorities (TDAs).}
Independent TDAs each hold one Threshold EC-ElGamal
decryption share and collaboratively decrypt authenticated
aggregate ciphertexts only after successful verification.
At least \(t\) of \(n\) TDAs are required to recover an
aggregate result.

\item \textbf{Trusted Authority (TA).}
The TA acts as the root of trust during system
initialization by generating public parameters, issuing
attribute secret keys, initializing Threshold EC-ElGamal,
distributing threshold decryption shares, provisioning
cryptographic credentials, and publishing the public
parameters. It is not involved in searchable query
processing, aggregation, or result verification after
setup.

\end{enumerate}

