\subsection{Threat Model}

We consider probabilistic polynomial-time (PPT)
adversaries that attempt to infer sensor values, recover
query semantics, violate access policies, manipulate
aggregation results, or tamper with authenticated query
states.

\subsubsection{Honest-but-Curious Edge Gateway}

The Edge Gateway faithfully performs sensor
authentication, authenticated index construction,
aggregation-metadata generation, and blockchain commitment
generation, but may attempt to infer information from
encrypted records and indexing metadata. Although it
observes searchable dimensions and canonical-node
memberships in \(Meta_i\), it possesses neither the ABSE
master secret, attribute secret keys, nor any threshold
decryption share, and therefore cannot decrypt sensor
records or aggregation ciphertexts. The Edge Gateway is
assumed to be non-colluding with the cloud server;
otherwise, compromise of the bitmap-selector secret
\(K_{sel}\) may expose protected bitmap indexes but not
encrypted data, threshold decryption keys, or
blockchain-authenticated query states.

\subsubsection{Honest-but-Curious Cloud Server}

The cloud faithfully executes searchable query processing,
bitmap filtering, and homomorphic aggregation while
attempting to infer information from encrypted records,
trapdoors, protected bitmaps, and the leakage specified by
\(\mathcal{L}_{BVCRSA}\). It may additionally attempt to
omit, modify, replay, or fabricate query responses;
however, such attacks are detected through
blockchain-anchored query states, authenticated batch
commitments, and Merkle-proof verification. The cloud
cannot decrypt encrypted data, generate unauthorized
trapdoors, or forge authenticated query states.

\subsubsection{Unauthorized Users and Threshold Decryption Authorities}

Unauthorized users may attempt to generate valid
trapdoors, bypass access policies, or collude with the
cloud. Aggregate ciphertexts are protected by Threshold
EC-ElGamal, where each Threshold Decryption Authority
(TDA) holds a single decryption share. We assume fewer
than \(t\) of \(n\) TDAs are compromised; consequently,
no coalition of the cloud, users, and fewer than \(t\)
TDAs can recover plaintext aggregates or the aggregation
private key.

\subsubsection{Blockchain and Deployment Assumptions}

Adversaries may attempt rollback, replay, message
tampering, omission, or aggregation-result forgery.
Blockchain-anchored commitments, authenticated batch
commitments, Merkle proofs, AES-GCM, and randomized ABSE
trapdoors provide integrity, freshness, and authenticated
query verification.

The Trusted Authority (TA) is trusted only during system
initialization for parameter generation, credential
issuance, and threshold-share distribution. The underlying
cryptographic primitives, Threshold EC-ElGamal, and the
permissioned blockchain are assumed secure, with only
finalized blockchain states accepted for verification.
Attacks that compromise the TA during initialization,
rewrite finalized blockchain states, collusion among at
least \(t\) TDAs, and physical, denial-of-service, or
side-channel attacks are outside the scope of this work.

